{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the asymmetry of outcomes, particularly in terms of the distribution of losses and gains. A lottery is right-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable (negative) outcomes relative to favorable (positive) ones. In this decision problem, both lotteries have a positive probability of zero (no loss) and a negative outcome (loss) with the remaining probability. Since both lotteries have only two outcomes — a loss and zero — the skewness can be assessed by comparing the probability of the loss (negative outcome) to the probability of zero. A higher probability of loss indicates greater right-skewness. Lottery A has a 20% chance of losing 4000 Euros and 80% chance of 0. Lottery B has a 25% chance of losing 3000 Euros and 75% chance of 0. Thus, Lottery A has a higher probability of a large loss (4000 vs. 3000), and since the loss is more severe and occurs with a higher probability (20% vs. 25%), it is more right-skewed in terms of the distribution of losses. However, the decision reason specifies that it prefers the lottery that is more right-skewed. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery A over Lottery B. The information relevant to the decision reason is thus present: the probabilities and magnitudes of losses and zero outcomes are available and can be used to assess skewness.",
  "step_2": "The individual’s verbal report states: 'Harte Entscheidung, aber da die Wahrscheinlichkeiten des Verlusts wieder sehr nah beieinander liegen, wähle ich die Möglichkeit mit dem geringeren Verlust.' This translates to: 'A hard decision, but since the probabilities of loss are very close to each other, I choose the option with the smaller loss.' The individual acknowledges that the probabilities of loss are close (20% vs. 25%), and based on that, chooses the lottery with the smaller loss (Lottery B, which has a 3000 Euro loss vs. 4000 Euro loss). The individual does not mention skewness, loss magnitude in terms of distribution, or any asymmetry in the outcomes. Instead, the reasoning is based solely on the magnitude of the loss and the proximity of the loss probabilities. The individual focuses on the smaller loss (3000 vs. 4000), not on the distribution of outcomes or skewness. Thus, the considered information is limited to loss size and the closeness of loss probabilities, with no reference to the shape or skewness of the outcome distribution.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the distribution of outcomes, the probability of losses relative to gains, or any asymmetry in the outcomes. Instead, the individual bases the choice on the magnitude of the loss and the proximity of the loss probabilities, which reflects a different decision rule — namely, minimizing the expected loss or choosing the less severe loss. The decision reason, which prefers the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., one with a higher probability of a large loss), is not reflected in the reasoning. In fact, Lottery A is more right-skewed (20% chance of a 4000 Euro loss), but the individual chooses Lottery B (smaller loss), which is not consistent with the skewness-based reason. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The reasoning is distinct and does not align with the rule specified in the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
